@hair.soupp To me, the strongest and most logical pro choice argument is bodily autonomy, period. We do not force human beings to use their bodies to keep other human beings alive. We just don’t. We can’t force people to donate blood, bone marrow, kidneys, organs, nothing. We can’t even take organs from a DEAD person to save lives unless they explicitly agreed to it beforehand. Think about that for a second. We literally give dead corpses more bodily autonomy than some people want to give living women.

And before people start screaming about “9 month abortions,” let’s be serious. The idea that women are carrying wanted pregnancies all the way through hellish physical exhaustion, baby showers, nursery planning, diapers, names picked out, maternity leave arranged, etc., and then just randomly deciding at the last second “nah never mind” is honestly absurd and insulting. The vast majority of late term abortions are heartbreaking medical situations involving wanted babies and devastated parents.

You can personally believe someone SHOULD use their body to keep another human alive. Fine. But bodily autonomy means that no person, no government, and no law has the right to force another human being to use their body against their will, even in the name of saving a life.
